<article class="env-remark" id="01YA">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01YA">Remark <span data-tag="01YA">5.4.7.7</span>.</a> The existence of morphisms between $\infty $-bicategories which do not preserve thin $2$-simplices should be viewed as a feature of our formalism, rather than a bug. Recall that, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ are bicategories, then Theorem <a data-tag="00AU" href="/tag/00AU">2.3.4.1</a> supplies a bijection </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \{  \textnormal{Strictly unital lax functors $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$} \}  \ar [d]^-{\sim } \\ \{  \textnormal{Morphisms of simplicial sets $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{D}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{D}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$} \} . } \]
</div>
<p> Consequently, we can think of general morphisms of simplicial sets as providing a generalization of the notion of (strictly) unital lax functors to the setting of $\infty $-bicategories. </p>
</article>
